00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.210
As a matter of fact, probably
as a quick side note,

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.180
the most interesting thing for
me in the voice space is that

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.170
we have not established
kind of the UI and the UX

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.130
and the interface of
how we're gonna interact

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.740
with these things as they become

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.830
dramatically more sophisticated.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.230
That comes along with empathy
and respect for the end user.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.150
You being at this conference all day

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.180
the empathy and respect
I have for you is that

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.760
we have a very detailed curriculum
and line up at this event

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.970
where I think a lot of
people are gonna nerd it out

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.610
and so I'm not gonna nerd it out.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.300
I'm gonna go on a little bit
of a different direction.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.130
I wanna set up the framework
of why this event even exists

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.210
and why, for many of you if you follow me

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:23.280
in any shape or form, are
hearing me pontificate

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.560
and push and propaganda this space.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.280
It comes down to, it was interesting,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.180
Matt Guerin, who's gonna
do a great job after me

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.650
here from Vayner, asked me literally,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.870
or Kim just asked me, Matt
just asked me actually,

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.930
he said what are you gonna talk about.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.130
I looked at him like I
have no fucking idea.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.720
And it was really interesting
because the reality is

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.720
I don't but I do and I do
because of the following.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.710
In my career at this point, in my life,

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.870
it's been interesting to
watch pattern recognition.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.970
As I was sitting back there
getting ready for this talk

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:04.120
the only feeling and kind of
thought and vibe that I had

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.210
was very similar to the vibe I had

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.730
the first time I was sitting in front of

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.570
seven liquor store owners.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.020
My dad's liquor store was part of a co-op

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.130
where there were other owners

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.390
and they all kind of owned
Shoppers Discount Liquors.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:18.940
They were independent but they were buying

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.170
an advertising co-op and I
remember going to a meeting

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.030
in 1996 where I was going to let them know

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.930
that I was starting a website.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.550
And we're in this meeting
and I'm like yeah,

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.370
we're gonna launch this
thing and we're gonna call it

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:35.970
winelibrary.com, which
was already a little weird

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.250
'cause we were Shoppers Discount Liquors.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.230
And I just remember the enormous scrutiny

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.830
and almost disrespect
through everybody's faces

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.430
because basically what the
energy of that room told me

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:54.220
is that nobody's gonna buy
wine from this internet thing.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.470
I genuinely believed that
more than half that room

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.300
had no idea what the internet was, right.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.300
And so when I sit here today,
this is a pretty fancy event,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.560
meaning not only are the
speakers pretty educated

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.410
and far along with technology and business

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.000
but so is the audience itself.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.580
I've been thinking a lot, I'm like huh,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.380
it's really quite interesting
that nobody in this room,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.010
including myself,
actually fully understands

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.290
how deep this is gonna go and
here's what I mean by that.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.820
I think it's very hard for us
in this room, at this moment,

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.690
at this apex of text messaging
and mobile addiction,

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.500
I think it's hard for us to understand

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.010
that there's a high
likelihood that in 10 years,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.720
that so many of you are
gonna give up on texting

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.300
for voice AI devices.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.530
It's just hard to make the leap that

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.880
what you've now become completely
foundationally living on

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.660
as your execution of
communication, as a platform,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.010
is going to be disrupted.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.400
No different, it's funny, I
love when young becomes old.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.410
I love that there's so many
people in here that I know

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.520
and so many of my friends that are now

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.520
in their 30s, 40s, 50s, who
were such thought leaders

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:14.520
20 years ago, 25 years ago,
about digital and social

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.970
and the internet itself,
who now are gonna struggle

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.110
with this transition.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.560
Who don't think they're
gonna stop texting.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.160
In the same way that
they made fun of people

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.790
who didn't think they were
gonna text a decade ago.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.480
And so I think, for everybody here,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.530
I think there's a lot
of entrepreneurs here

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.880
and a lot of Fortune 500's here

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.630
and I think the theme for me is

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.980
you're about to hear a
lot of incredible data.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.240
Depending on the study you look at

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.923
when you look at 50 to 100
million devices in home already,

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.730
AI voice devices in the marketplace,

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:50.830
when you think about how
many more that's going to be

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.760
in the 12 to 24 months
but more importantly,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.130
for the people that are advanced

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.500
in their technology thinking,

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.560
when you know in a half
decade it's not even gonna be

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.720
those devices and it's
gonna be integrated into

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.270
every single product in our lives.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.850
Including, when your shower
head is voice activated

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.210
and you remember something
that you need to do

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.920
and you just tell somebody
you're gonna be 10 minutes late

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.860
or what's my meeting tonight at seven.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.950
I don't think that we have quantified

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:17.900
our unbelievable addiction for speed.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.700
Everything that happens,
pretty much, I'm a simple guy.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.010
I talk a lot about day trading attention.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.690
A lot of it is just
predicating the fact that

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.470
you guys hate friction.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.360
That we as a society give
up everything, privacy.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.340
We will give up privacy all
day to win back three minutes.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.330
We just love speed.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.070
We subconsciously understand the time.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:40.970
You hear that saying time is, right.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.820
We subconsciously, deeply understand that.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.620
To me it was really fascinating early on

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:51.240
to see Uber succeed, watching
Travis navigate that business

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.830
and later as an early investor.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.320
What struck me so early on is like

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.830
these people can't afford it.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.110
I was looking at some
of the data and I'm like

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.050
they can't afford it.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.990
Why are they taking these $28,
right, they can't afford it.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.060
And what you just start realizing is

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.580
convenience is king.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:09.690
I grew up in retail.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.350
Convenience is king is super-duper real.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.780
There is nothing more convenient

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:19.680
than voice infrastructure
around us, period.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.090
This space is going to
win and it's going to win

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.570
in a big game and in a big way.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:29.270
This space is also
unbelievably fascinating

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.130
because it is not visual.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.520
Let me explain to you why this matters.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.070
I'm gonna paint you a scenario.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.950
When you think about
the search engine wars

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.700
of the early 2000s, even
when Google won and became

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.730
a real dominant force and they
started having an ad product

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.060
and people started
freaking out how powerful

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.630
search engines were, when
somebody would type in Coca-Cola

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:53.340
and Pepsi had the ability to have an ad.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.430
Even to this day, in the small
screen of a mobile device,

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.250
even when somebody buys your category

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:03.140
or your brand as an ad, you're
still showing up organically

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:07.010
in the UI of the visual and
if I'm typing in Reebok,

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:08.580
even though there may be a Nike ad,

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.270
if my intent was Reebok
and even though I might be

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.790
seduced for a second by that Nike ad,

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.760
my intent going in was still that

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.400
and I have the ability to buy that.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.960
What's fascinating to me is

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.500
we do not have that luxury in voice.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.030
What's fascinating to me about voice is

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:29.030
the players that win this
game, Amazon, Google, Apple.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.350
Facebook, I assume, has to
be doing something secretly

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.175
in some fucking secret warehouse.

00:08:35.175 --> 00:08:37.090
(audience laughing)

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:38.520
And anybody else that we can't see,

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.380
whether it's a China based
Tencent or somebody else

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.300
or something else that is just

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.120
two 18 year old girls
right now in Tennessee

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.230
who have an idea and are
able to put it together

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.140
and get there.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.230
Whoever it's going to be,
they're going to have remarkable,

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.830
and I mean remarkable leverage.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.680
To me the internet is the
foundation of our society

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.790
and then you've got four
to seven internet companies

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.300
that are layers on top of that

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.010
and then for everyone of
us here our job is to be

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.020
the next layer above that.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.000
These tolbooths, Google,
Amazon, Apple, in voice,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.800
are set to make so much more money

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.690
and have so much more power.

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.440
It's also why, for the people.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.110
How many people here run
or think they have a brand,

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:21.063
raise your hands.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.360
So this is super important to me.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.870
In the internet age we have
had incredibly fascinating

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.240
confusion between selling and branding.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.650
I listen to people everyday
talk about business

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.220
and they don't realize that
they're just sales people

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:39.600
but they're talking like
they're marketing or branding.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.200
What they care about is CAC and LTV

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:46.200
and last touch attribution
and really just hacking,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.460
math based marketing,
which I'm a huge fan of.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.360
I just think it's half of the equation.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.000
I think the art and the
creative is the other half

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.270
and I've spent the last
decade being fascinated by

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:58.340
Wall Street and Madison Avenue

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.010
just being completely
based on subjective art

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:04.320
and bullshit reporting and
then Silicon Valley being

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.240
disproportionately addicted to math

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.540
and not realizing that
that eventually runs out

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.700
and that becomes their vulnerability.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.370
To me the thing that I'm
probably most passionate about

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:18.200
with voice is the fact
that brand has never become

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:19.350
more important.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.630
Every single person here is going to buy

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.680
an obnoxious amount of
products, goods and services

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.880
on a voice device in a decade.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:29.820
And I mean most of it.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.050
When that happens you
will start realizing why

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.310
things like Kleenex and Xerox and Uber

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:38.290
were the most important players.

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:41.460
If you do not start the process right now

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:45.570
of branding Chips Ahoy over cookies,

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.110
what's going to happen
is we're just gonna say

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:52.050
Alexa or Google, send me
some chocolate chip cookies.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.890
And our battle is going to be Alexa,

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.230
send me some Chips Ahoy.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:01.230
Because I promise you, 'cause
Amazon is a fucking gangster.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:04.860
If you say Chips Ahoy
you've gotta get those

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:08.430
but if you say cookies you're
getting Amazon's private label

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.340
and or Mondelez in that scenario

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:14.350
is paying a quadrillion dollars.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.823
I laugh when I meet with my CPG clients.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.550
They're concerned about
the amount of money

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.230
that have to pour into trade.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:22.580
And for the entrepreneurs in this room

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:26.290
that means good end caps
at Walmart when you walk in

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:27.770
and how much they extract.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.730
Or when they're on deal
they have to subsidize

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.070
the coupon price.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:34.450
That's gonna be child's
play compared to being

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.190
the default product when somebody asks for

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.470
a category product.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:42.250
When you ask for jeans how
much is Calvin Klein or Levis

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:44.000
gonna have to pay to be that product

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.930
verus the margin that Amazon makes

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:51.270
in having their own private
label, versus Google going out

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:54.600
and buying Levis and owning it

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:56.370
and making that the default.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:59.370
My friends, I think the
Amazon purchase of wholefoods

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.110
was a watershed moment that
finally got people to understand

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.200
that this is just starting.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.280
That these companies
have a lot of leverage.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:09.630
When target sells to Amazon
or Facebook or Google

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.740
people are gonna start waking
up to what's happening.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:15.740
And the thing that's
fascinating to me is that voice,

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.950
in a lot of ways, has
that parallel impact.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.440
I do not think people understand

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.180
how much voice is gonna affect

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.100
the entire restaurant industry.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:29.940
There is no OpenTable or Resy or Yelp.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:31.173
There's voice.

00:12:32.411 --> 00:12:34.240
And the smarts is something I think

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:35.220
a lot of people are gonna struggle with.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.750
How many people here saw
the Google presentation

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.164
with the assistant making the reservation,

00:12:40.164 --> 00:12:40.997
just raise it high.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.690
What do you think that technology
looks like in seven years?

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:47.523
Right.

00:12:47.523 --> 00:12:49.610
I mean this shit is getting
continuously smarter.

